A='Opening';B='The moving body';C='The printed witness';E='Messages from elsewhere';F='The measuring room';G='Exit gallery'
add('threshold',A,1,'hero','United States · 1820–1861','The Uncertain Witness','Antebellum Gothic Science',
'''Imagine that a doctor tells you a dead man spoke. What is the first thing you want to know? Hold on to your question. A reader in 1845 could encounter just such a report in a magazine. This exhibition follows the evidence that made claims about life, death, and identity persuasive in the decades before the Civil War.
Here, “Gothic science” names an encounter between experimental inquiry and stories of dread, bodily vulnerability, and uncertain knowledge. It is our interpretive term. The people in these rooms called their own work chemistry, animal magnetism, literature, Spiritualism, or ethnology.''','poe ogden mit',img='lens',cue='Invite students to name one thing they would ask the doctor.')
add('voice',A,2,'cinema','New York · Literary fiction · 1845','“I say to you that I am dead!”','Who could verify a statement like this?',
'''In Edgar Allan Poe’s story, the speaker is Ernest Valdemar, a dying man who agrees to be mesmerized. The narrator reports hearing distinctly formed words long after Valdemar appears to have died. If speech is evidence of life, what are listeners supposed to make of the sentence?
Poe gives a philosophical puzzle a physical setting. The patient’s voice reaches us through the person conducting the experiment. That narrator chooses what counts as a sign of life and which details we receive. Keep track of that double role as we move from real experiments to fictional ones.''','poe',img='sickroom',cue='Read the line aloud once. Give students time to identify the voice they actually have access to.')
add('question',A,2,'manifest','The historical question','When did seeing become believing?','In antebellum America, a twitch, a printed case, and a column of figures could command belief. Each also gave someone the power to interpret another person’s body.',
'''The central argument concerns authority. Public demonstrations and print made more people into witnesses, yet control over what an audience saw, who got named, and how a result was classified remained uneven. Gothic writers exposed the distance between an observer’s confident voice and what that observer could know. Black critics exposed the political stakes of that distance.
By the end, use one primary source to distinguish an observation from an inference. Explain how a European practice changed in an American setting. Then compare two cases to show whose authority increased and whose testimony was discounted.''','ogden poe doug',items=[('Observe','Separate a sign from an inference.'),('Trace','Follow an idea across the Atlantic.'),('Compare','Ask who gained authority.')])
add('frog',B,2,'plate','Bologna · 1791 · European prologue','The twitch on the table','A prepared frog’s leg contracted when its nerve was electrically stimulated. Where did the electricity come from?',
'''Read Galvani’s engraving as a sequence of things to look at. The prepared limb lets the experimenter observe muscle movement separately from the behavior of a whole frog. The apparatus and conductors let a reader imagine repeating or varying the conditions. The plate gathers several arrangements into a single image.
Luigi Galvani understood the results as evidence for electricity within animals. Alessandro Volta emphasized electricity arising from contact between different metals in certain setups. The disagreement concerned the source of the effect. In modern terms, electrical excitation can cause a muscle to contract. This finding settles a question about tissue, while leaving open what counts as the life of an organism.''','gal whip',img='galvani')
add('eye',B,2,'pair','London · 1803','An eye opened. What followed?','Giovanni Aldini applied electricity to the body of an executed man. The jaw moved and an eye opened.',
'''Aldini, Galvani’s nephew, demonstrated electrical stimulation on the body of George Forster in London. His published account drew attention to the appearance of reanimation. Resuscitation mattered in an age in which a person pulled from water could appear dead. The prospect of saving an apparently drowned person helped give the research urgency.
Contraction of facial muscles can explain the visible movement. A spectator who attributed awareness to the eye supplied an inference the demonstration could not establish. Notice who could object to that inference: the audience could speak, while the executed man was available to the anatomists under the penal system.''','ald electric',items=[('A defensible observation','Electrical stimulation produced movement.'),('A further claim','The man could see the people around him.')],response='Only the first claim follows from the observation. An open eye tells us about the position of an eyelid. Evidence of perception would require a different test.',cue='Invite a vote on which claim a spectator could defend. Ask for the missing evidence before revealing the response.')
add('gleason',B,3,'wide','New England · 1836','A mill worker made the demonstration','Charles Poyen proposed an invisible magnetic influence. Audiences came to watch what Cynthia Gleason could do.',
'''Animal magnetism was a European theory that a practitioner could influence another person through an invisible agency, often described as a fluid. Electricity made invisible natural forces seem plausible, but the two practices had different evidentiary histories. A subject’s response could be genuine without demonstrating a magnetic fluid.
Poyen began lecturing in Boston in 1836. His demonstrations drew greater attention after he partnered with Cynthia Ann Gleason, a Pawtucket textile worker. Her responses in a state described as magnetic sleep gave audiences something to see. Historian Emily Ogden places these performances in a region where employers were already concerned with directing workers’ attention and conduct. This reading helps explain the appeal of a practice that promised influence over a will.''','poy quinn lowell ogden',img='poyen')
add('certificate',B,2,'quotation','Boston · 1837 · Title-page evidence','“from the most respectable sources.”','Charles Poyen advertised his book’s witnesses in its title. What kind of qualification was respectability?',
'''Look at the three kinds of evidence Poyen put together: experiments, reports, and certificates. Experiments suggested the possibility of repetition. Reports carried accounts beyond the room. A certificate lent a recognizable name to an endorsement. In Lowell, Poyen emphasized the standing of Alexander Wright, an agent of a carpet factory.
Consider the division of labor. Gleason’s performance made something visible. Men with positions in the community could certify what readers were invited to believe about it. We have more evidence here of Poyen’s strategy for earning trust than of Gleason’s private interpretation of her experience.''','poy lowell ogden',img='poyen',response='Respectability described social standing. A signature can establish that someone endorsed a report. It does not establish the physical cause of an observed response.')
add('case',C,3,'plate','American Review · December 1845','Poe builds a case file','A time of death. Physicians’ initials. A student taking notes. Which of these can the reader inspect independently?',
'''Poe’s narrator first poses a research question: could mesmerism arrest death? He obtains Valdemar’s agreement and describes clinicians attending him. Details of elapsed time and a medical student’s note-taking make the procedure sound accountable. The patient appears suspended for nearly seven months. When the narrator tries to wake him, his body disintegrates.
Examine the type on the original page. The heading calls it a case and identifies Edgar A. Poe as author. Everything inside the account, including the purported witnesses, reaches the reader through one fictional narrator. The device lets readers experience how easily the familiar form of a report can stand in for access to its evidence.''','poe poescan mabbott',img='valdemar',cue='Ask students to identify the most persuasive detail and explain why they chose it.')
add('reprint',C,2,'wide','New York to London · 1845–1846','One story acquires another witness','A London publisher advertised a “plain recital of facts.” A Scottish reader wrote to Poe to check.',
'''The December American Review called the story “The Facts of M. Valdemar’s Case.” The Broadway Journal printed it that month under the now familiar title, “The Facts in the Case of M. Valdemar.” A London pamphlet in 1846 surrounded the story with a promotional claim about its truth. Printing created a new occasion to believe the same account.
Specific readers left evidence of uncertainty. Archibald Ramsay, a Scottish pharmacist, sought clarification. Poe wrote back on December 30, 1846 and described the case as a hoax. The letter establishes one inquiry and an author’s answer. A claim about how many readers believed the story would require a different body of evidence.''','poe poelet mabbott andrio',img='type')
add('corroborate',C,3,'challenge','Evidence workshop · Pairs','Which source could corroborate the bedside account?','Choose one and explain what you would need to know about its origin.',
'''Try this as a historical counterfactual. Imagine you received an extraordinary case report of unknown origin. What could you look for beyond another printing of the same text? An independent source would need its own route from event to record. Even a separate document could turn out to have copied the first account.
Apply that test back to Poe. A named medical witness inside a fictional narrative is part of the narrative. A bookdealer’s description and a second edition are evidence of circulation. The distinction allows a historian to learn a great deal about publication without treating Valdemar as a patient who actually underwent the experiment.''','poe andrio',items=[('A','A second printing of the same account'),('B','A separately preserved bedside observer’s notebook'),('C','A publisher’s claim that readers believe it')],response='B could provide independent testimony if the notebook were authentic, contemporary, and based on direct observation. A and C trace the story’s circulation. No such independent notebook establishes Poe’s fictional event.',cue='Allow one minute to choose, one to defend, and one to compare answers.')
add('birthmark',C,2,'cinema','Nathaniel Hawthorne · Fiction · 1843','The treatment succeeds. Georgiana dies.','In “The Birth-mark,” who decided that the mark on her cheek needed a cure?',
'''Hawthorne published the tale in 1843 and set it late in the eighteenth century. Aylmer comes to regard the small hand-shaped mark on his wife Georgiana’s face as an intolerable sign of human imperfection. Georgiana comes to share his distress and drinks his preparation. As the mark fades, she dies.
Poe asks what gives an account the sound of truth. Hawthorne asks what makes an experiment worth attempting in the first place. Aylmer obtains the visible result he wanted, at the cost of the person he supposedly wished to help. Georgiana’s agreement matters to the story, as does the fact that her husband taught her to see the mark as a defect.''','haw',img='birthmark',cue='Ask whose definition of success would count if Georgiana lived with the mark.')
add('wire',E,2,'tape','Washington to Baltimore · May 24, 1844','A message leaves a mark','“What hath God wrought?”',
'''Samuel F. B. Morse sent the biblical phrase from Washington to Alfred Vail in Baltimore. Annie Ellsworth had suggested the words from Numbers 23:23. The receiver registered marks on a paper strip that an operator could interpret. The handwriting on the surviving artifact helps identify the occasion. A written label and the instrument’s marks serve different purposes.
The phrase framed technical achievement in religious language. Telegraphy also offered a material model for distant presence: a reply could arrive from someone the receiver could not see. Operators could compare a message at each end of a known circuit. Keep that check in mind when the language of the wire enters a different setting.''','morse',img='morse-tape')
add('rap',E,2,'tele','Hydesville, New York · 1848','A knock becomes an answer','Kate and Maggie Fox became known as mediums after their family interpreted raps as replies from the dead.',
'''At the Fox home, sounds took on the meaning of answers in an exchange. The claim depended on identifying an unseen speaker. Hearing a noise, discerning a pattern, and attributing it to a particular dead person are three distinct steps. They call for different kinds of evidence.
Spiritualism offered adherents the possibility of continuing relationships with the dead. Witnessing and testing, alongside religious conviction, mattered to many participants. The New York weekly The Spiritual Telegraph began in 1852. Its name shows how readily the language of a working communications technology could describe a spiritual hope. A title can document that analogy without proving a message’s origin.''','fox tele morse',img='spiritual')
add('hare',E,2,'plate','Philadelphia chemist · New York publication · 1855','Robert Hare wanted a mechanical witness','Could a dial answer a question while a medium could not see its letters?',
'''Hare had built electrical apparatus and taught chemistry at the University of Pennsylvania. His training left him practiced at turning a difficult-to-see effect into a public demonstration. In retirement he brought the same habits to investigating mediumship. In his 1855 book he described apparatus intended to separate a medium’s actions from a message or reading.
Read the drawing as Hare’s proposed control. A barrier to seeing letters could address one way of selecting them. It could not by itself identify a spirit as the author of a message. Hare came to believe that he had established communication with the dead. His movement between chemistry and Spiritualism helps explain why a simple opposition between science and faith misses the historical question.''','battery hare',img='hare')
add('sender',E,2,'pair','Evidence workshop · Compare','How would you identify the sender?','A telegraph operator and a séance participant each receive what appears to be an answer.',
'''Pretend you have been asked to evaluate the two claims. For the wire, an operator at one station can choose a word privately and another can record the received word. Repetition with new words tests whether information traveled. The circuit, key, register, and operators can all be examined.
At a séance, receiving expected answers could feel personally significant. To attribute them to a particular deceased person, an investigator would need to exclude ways the living participants could already know or signal the answers. Even an unexplained result would leave its source to be established. The point is to ask what each test can decide.''','morse fox hare',items=[('Telegraph','Compare a privately chosen message at both stations.'),('Mediumship','What would rule out information supplied by a living participant?')],response='Agreement between two records can demonstrate transmission over a known wire. An unexplained reply at a séance leaves the identity of its sender open. The religious meaning of the encounter is a separate historical question.')
add('measure',F,2,'wide','Philadelphia · 1839','Who chose the categories?','In Crania Americana, Samuel George Morton sorted human skulls into five presumed races and compared their internal volumes.',
'''Morton treated racial categories as preexisting divisions and organized his collection accordingly. For his 1839 volume he estimated cranial capacity using seed. A measure of capacity in cubic inches could be repeated and corrected. The categories, the choice of people whose remains were collected, and the jump from capacity to intellectual rank each required a separate justification.
This was scientific racism: the use of scientific methods or prestige to naturalize racial hierarchy. A reader could mistake an orderly table for a demonstration that unequal rights reflected nature. Antebellum Black thinkers disputed both the purported evidence and its political uses. One of them will read alongside us.''','mort mit doug',img='morton')
add('table',F,4,'table','Primary-source lab · Crania Americana, p. 260','Read past the averages','Morton’s five groups have different averages. Find a volume that falls inside every reported range.',
'''Use the lowest and highest values in each original row. Reading across the ranges shows that 80 cubic inches fits every row; in fact, the intervals share 75 through 89. This is a statement about reported ranges. The table does not list how often each exact value occurred. The groups also contain very different numbers of people.
The column of means makes a ranked list easy to see. There is no column measuring intellectual performance. Scholars disagree about particular sources of error in Morton’s measurements. The argument about racial intelligence fails even if every reported volume is accepted. Historical racial labels appear here solely to identify how Morton constructed the rows.''','mort mit',img='gothic-morton-table',response='80 cubic inches fits all five reported intervals. One is based on only 10 people and another on 147. The overlap undermines any attempt to identify a person’s group from that volume. No column measures intelligence.',cue='Let pairs choose a value, verify it against all five rows, and name the unmeasured trait.')
add('collection',F,2,'manifest','The collection behind the table','Before a skull could be measured, someone had to take it.','Collectors, military officers, and physicians sent human remains to Morton. Access to burial places helped determine who became a specimen.',
'''The table encourages us to imagine a neutral sample assembled expressly to answer a question. The collection had a much rougher geography. Research tracing Indigenous ancestors in Morton’s collection documents grave removal and acquisition amid military conflict and settler expansion. Those networks helped determine which bodies entered the book and which stories were recorded about them.
A skull’s catalog entry might give a collector’s name and a speculative group label while omitting the person’s own name. The omission matters twice. It limits the evidence available for any generalization, and it records an imbalance of power over the dead. A history of evidence therefore has to ask how the material was acquired.''','penn mort mit',items=[('Acquisition','Who had access to burial places?'),('Attribution','Whose name survived in the record?')])
add('douglass',F,3,'plate','Western Reserve College, Ohio · July 12, 1854','Douglass cross-examines the author','“Ignorance, malice or prejudice, may disqualify a witness, and why not an author?”',
'''Frederick Douglass, who had escaped slavery and become an abolitionist editor and speaker, addressed a college audience on ethnology, the study and classification of peoples. In the printed address he names Morton. He asks readers to examine how ethnologists selected faces and bodies to stand for whole populations.
Douglass pointed to a circular habit: when an intellectually accomplished Black person complicated a stereotype, a writer could discount that person’s Black ancestry. That maneuver protected the rule by redefining its exceptions. Douglass drew on variation among individuals and on examples that hostile portrayals omitted. His argument shows that nineteenth-century critics could challenge scientific racism using evidence available in their own time.''','doug mort mit',img='douglass',response='Test the author as you would a courtroom witness. Ask who chose the illustrations, who is missing, and what evidence the author would accept as a counterexample.',cue='Have students apply Douglass’s question to one specific column in Morton’s table.')
add('compare',G,2,'matrix','Return to the opening question','What did each witness establish?','Separate the record that survives from the claim made on its behalf.',
'''Galvani’s experiments support a physical claim about the response of tissue to stimulation. Poe’s tale and a reader’s inquiry establish that a fictional case could invite a factual question. The Spiritual Telegraph establishes how a movement described its aspirations. Morton’s table documents an attempt to rank people and the choices that went into that ranking.
These are different kinds of evidence. The fact that a phantom sender and a racial hierarchy were both asserted does not make the stakes identical. One promise concerned reunion with the dead. Another gave authority to political subordination. Ask what a mistaken inference could cost and who would bear that cost.''','gal poelet tele mit doug',items=[('An experimental plate','A muscle can respond to stimulation.'),('A letter to a reader','Poe acknowledged the fiction.'),('A newspaper title','Believers borrowed the language of the wire.'),('A racial table','A collector sorted and measured people.')])
add('label',G,3,'challenge','Exit exercise · 60–80 words','Write the missing museum label','Choose two objects. Explain what each preserves, what someone inferred, and who stood to gain or lose if that inference won acceptance.',
'''Try writing without looking at the earlier screens. Name an object, place it in time, and identify one detail a visitor could actually inspect. Use the second object to make a comparison with stakes. Check the original page or the reading notes, then revise any phrase that makes a sweeping claim about what everyone believed.
A successful label makes a historical argument rather than issuing a verdict on a whole century. It gives the reader enough specific evidence to follow the inference. Different object pairings can support different answers.''','poy doug mit',items=[('01','Identify two dated objects.'),('02','Distinguish each observation from its interpretation.'),('03','Name a consequence for a particular person or group.')],response='Poyen’s 1837 title page advertises the standing of witnesses to a mill worker’s performance. Morton’s 1839 table presents group averages as if categories could speak for themselves. Both made bodily observations portable in print. The certificate elevated those who could vouch for an event. The table gave racial hierarchy the appearance of numerical proof, a strategy Douglass challenged by asking how its authors chose their examples.')
add('return',G,1,'hero closing','Exit gallery','Who gets the last word?','Valdemar speaks, but his examiner writes the record. Douglass asks readers to put the examiner on the stand.',
'''Return to the first question you wanted to ask the doctor. Has it changed? Perhaps you first wanted to know whether the patient was alive. Now you might also ask who examined him, whose record survives, and which finding would change the examiner’s mind.
The Gothic force of these stories lies partly in the possibility that a witness can report an effect accurately and still misunderstand the person before him. In antebellum public life, that gap could become a source of imaginative pleasure, religious hope, or political injury. Reading historically means distinguishing those consequences as carefully as the evidence that produced them.''','poe doug',img='lens')
